{
  "dom_id": "step-q269498",
  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "Consider the function $L:{R}^{3\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b),$ where\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td rowspan=\"3\" align=\"center\">\n${∇}_{A}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n\n<td rowspan=\"3\" width=\"8px\"></td>\n\n<td width=\"45px\" height=\"35px\" align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n\n<td width=\"45px\" height=\"35px\" align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n\n<td rowspan=\"3\" width=\"8px\"></td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td height=\"35px\" align=\"center\">$-32$</td>\n\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td height=\"35px\" align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n\n<td align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>",
  "worked_solution": "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWe can compute $\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}$ by applying the chain rule.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b\\in {R}^{3}.$ Then, $L(A)={y}^{T}y$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={\\sum }_{k=1}^{3}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{∇}_{y}L={∇}_{y}({y}^{T}y)=2y,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}=2{y}_{k}.\n$$\n\nWriting out $y=Ax-b$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\n{y}_{i}={\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{x}_{j}-{b}_{i},\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}={x}_{j}$ if $i=k$, and\n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=0$ if $i\\ne k.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{3}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $(i,j)$th entry of ${∇}_{A}L$ is $2$ times the $i$th entry of $y$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $x$, so\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=2y\\cdot {x}^{T}.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating ${∇}_{A}L$ at $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{A}L & =2y\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =2(Ax-b)\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =2([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2([\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -32 & -32 \\\\ 16 & 16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
